(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-antihypertensives"]={"title":"Antihypertensives Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug causes a DRY COUGH from bradykinin accumulating in the lungs?","choices":["Lisinopril","Losartan","Amlodipine","Metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors stop bradykinin breakdown; the cough is not dose- or agent-related. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient cannot tolerate an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's cough. Which drug is the SWITCH?","choices":["Enalapril","Valsartan","Captopril","Ramipril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not cause cough and have a lower incidence of angioedema, so patients may be switched to them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has an INTRAVENOUS active form?","choices":["Lisinopril","Benazepril","Enalapril","Quinapril"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enalapril is converted to enalaprilat, which is the intravenous form. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has a SHORT half-life, the exception to once-daily dosing?","choices":["Lisinopril","Ramipril","Trandolapril","Captopril"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors are once daily; captopril's short half-life is the exception. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which drug raises POTASSIUM, especially with renal disease or a potassium-sparing diuretic?","choices":["Candesartan","Nifedipine","Doxazosin","Clonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers (like ACE inhibitors) cause hyperkalemia, most often with renal disease or potassium-sparing diuretics. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker treats PAROXYSMAL SUPRAVENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Felodipine","Nisoldipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Only the non-dihydropyridines slow atrioventricular conduction, so only they treat supraventricular tachycardia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is approved for SUBARACHNOID HEMORRHAGE?","choices":["Nifedipine","Nicardipine","Nimodipine","Isradipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nimodipine's approved indication is subarachnoid hemorrhage. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is available INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Amlodipine","Felodipine","Nimodipine","Nicardipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines are oral only, except nicardipine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker raises DIGOXIN levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit P-glycoprotein (raising digoxin) and CYP3A4 (raising three statins). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker RAISES SIMVASTATIN levels by inhibiting CYP3A4?","choices":["Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug is most likely to cause REFLEX TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines dilate arteries strongly and provoke rebound tachycardia; immediate-release forms are avoided in unstable angina. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective and given INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Acebutolol","Esmolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the second-generation, beta-1 selective agent marked for intravenous use. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which beta blocker's HIGH LIPID SOLUBILITY brings nightmares and depression?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Nadolol","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Highly lipid-soluble agents cause more central effects: depression, nightmares, vivid dreams and hallucinations. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which beta blocker ALSO BLOCKS ALPHA-1 and is used in heart failure?","choices":["Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol","Nadolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade, antioxidant and antiproliferative actions; it is a heart failure agent. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which beta blocker blocks alpha-1 AND acts as a BETA-2 AGONIST?","choices":["Propranolol","Bisoprolol","Labetalol","Esmolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labetalol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade and beta-2 agonist activity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is one of the three used for HEART FAILURE?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Timolol","Bisoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The heart failure beta blockers are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol, started very low and increased slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for GLAUCOMA, reducing aqueous humor production?","choices":["Timolol","Esmolol","Atenolol","Labetalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Timolol reduces aqueous humor production in the ciliary body; systemic effects of the drops are notable. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for MIGRAINE PROPHYLAXIS?","choices":["Esmolol","Propranolol","Bisoprolol","Atenolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol (with timolol) is used for migraine prophylaxis at low dose; the mechanism is uncertain. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is NON-SELECTIVE (first generation)?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Nadolol","Bisoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first-generation non-selective agents are nadolol, penbutolol, pindolol, propranolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which alpha-1 blocker is ALPHA-1A SELECTIVE, treating the prostate with little vascular effect?","choices":["Prazosin","Terazosin","Doxazosin","Tamsulosin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tamsulosin selectively blocks alpha-1A receptors; vascular receptors are alpha-1B, so blood pressure effects are limited. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which antihypertensive MILDLY LOWERS LDL (low-density lipoprotein) AND TRIGLYCERIDES and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Prazosin","Propranolol","Clonidine","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin shows evidence of a mild fall in LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides with a rise in HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which drug is a central ALPHA-2 AGONIST that BLUNTS OPIATE WITHDRAWAL?","choices":["Guanfacine","Clonidine","Prazosin","Hydralazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine's analgesic activity blunts opiate withdrawal reactions; it stimulates central alpha-2 and imidazoline receptors. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which central agent is the MOST ALPHA-2 SELECTIVE, with LESS SEDATION?","choices":["Clonidine","Prazosin","Guanfacine","Tamsulosin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guanfacine is less potent than clonidine, the most selective for alpha-2 over alpha-1, less sedating, with occasional withdrawal. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES BLOOD GLUCOSE by inhibiting insulin secretion and retains sodium?","choices":["Lisinopril","Amlodipine","Hydralazine","Clonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine increases blood glucose by inhibiting insulin secretion and causes sodium retention, so it is often given with a diuretic. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which drug is N-ACETYLATED, with LUPUS SYNDROME in slow acetylators?","choices":["Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nitroprusside","Prazosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine lupus syndrome risk: high dose, long term, women, slow acetylators and Caucasian ancestry. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which drug should come with the warning that STOOLS MAY TURN BLACK?","choices":["Minoxidil","Hydralazine","Clonidine","Verapamil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stools may turn black on hydralazine; the patient is told ahead of time. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which drug OPENS ATP-MODULATED POTASSIUM CHANNELS and causes HYPERTRICHOSIS?","choices":["Hydralazine","Nitroprusside","Minoxidil","Diltiazem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Minoxidil hyperpolarizes arteriolar smooth muscle through potassium efflux and grows hair on the face, back, arms and legs. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which drug given by INFUSION for HYPERTENSIVE CRISIS can cause CYANIDE toxicity?","choices":["Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nicardipine","Nitroprusside"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside carries five cyanide groups; sodium thiosulfate limits cyanide toxicity, and thiocyanate builds up with long infusions or renal failure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED in CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE and the ELDERLY because of reflex sympathetic workload?","choices":["Hydralazine","Metoprolol","Lisinopril","Amlodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine is contraindicated in coronary artery disease, the elderly and ischemia: vasodilation plus sympathetic activation raises cardiac work. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"}]};
